{
  "_comment": "Map for the game screen (2026-11-02). Positions are NOT typed in: geodata.py looks each place up on Wikidata by its search terms and only accepts a result whose label contains must_contain, that sits inside bbox, and that lies within max_m metres of the rough lat/lng estimate given here (the estimate is only an anchor for that check - it is never drawn). Places that can't be verified are left off the map, and the Map button only appears once enough places verify (geodata.VIABILITY_*). Layers: 'historic' is a georeferenced period street plan, used only if its tiles exist under static/ (build them with tools/georeference_scan.py); otherwise it opens on Esri's dark-gray canvas (few labels, no key needed), with a parchment-toned light-gray canvas as an option. CARTO's basemaps were dropped (2026-11-03): they now show an 'API key required' watermark; OpenTopoMap was dropped as too busy even when filtered. Each layer carries a CSS 'filter' (desaturate/tint/darken) so the base map stays in the background behind the markers instead of competing with them - central Petrograd's rivers, canals and street plan are largely unchanged since 1917. 2026-11-03: positions found by a manual pass over Wikidata (search, geosearch, SPARQL) and OpenStreetMap (Nominatim) are stored as `pinned` - with the source item, the matched name and a precision (building / street / quarter / district / town); those are used as-is and never looked up again. Litovsky and Keksgolmsky have no barracks entry in either source and are left unpinned (the server still tries the lookup).",
